{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Then the two men shall stand.</b> These are the witnesses. From here we learn that the witnesses must testify while standing, for their testimony is valid only in standing position, and women's testimony is not valid." "<b>Who have the strife.</b> These are the litigants. <b>Before HaShem.</b> Let it be accounted by you as though when you stand before the priests and the judges, it is as if you stand before the Holy One, blessed be He; as it is written (Psalms 82): ״בקרב אלהים ישפוט״. And it should have said לפני יהוה אלהיך, as is the manner of the entire passage, and of all of Deuteronomy. But the reason for the matter is that if it had said so, I would understand that all litigants among Israel who require judgment would all need to go from the other cities to the Great Court in Jerusalem and be judged there, for that is the meaning of לפני יהוה אלהיך, in the manner of (Deuteronomy 12:18): ״לפני יהוה אלהיך תאכלנו״. But this is not so, for it says ״אשר יהיו בימים ההם״, and it is the court in every single gate that Scripture speaks of. Therefore it was not possible to write לפני יהוה אלהיך, and it was necessary to write לפני ה׳ alone, to explain that the judge is called by the special Name. And this is why it says לפני ה׳ לפני הכהנים — 'before HaShem, before the priests' — and it did not say ולפני הכהנים, 'and before the priests,' for so we find that the righteous and the prophets are called by the special Name, as in the matter written concerning Moses (Exodus 33): ״והיה כל מבקש ה׳״, and likewise the angels are called in the Torah by the special Name, and I have already mentioned this several times."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {1 "The final sentence reads וכבר הזכרתי זה פעמים 'and I have already mentioned this several times'; retained exactly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:49:34.797991124Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}